1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,440
Where may a card number live in your system, and what does that one choice decide?

2
00:00:04,490 --> 00:00:09,000
Take a shop whose checkout keeps card numbers in its orders table, for refunds.

3
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:17,352
Reporting reads that table, a backup copies it every night, and every service and every developer's laptop can reach it.

4
00:00:17,402 --> 00:00:22,176
Ask where the card numbers are, and the honest answer is: everywhere the orders go.

5
00:00:22,226 --> 00:00:29,544
Every one of those systems now needs its network rules listed, its logins named, its logs kept for a year, and its changes controlled.

6
00:00:29,594 --> 00:00:35,256
A fee change waits for a card data review, and one weak laptop leaks every card.

7
00:00:35,306 --> 00:00:42,648
The standard itself gives the way out: keep card data in as few places as possible, and wall those places off.

8
00:00:42,698 --> 00:00:49,200
Then most of the system meets fewer requirements, and the part that meets all of them is small enough to get right.

9
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:59,952
P C I D S S is the card schemes' security standard for anyone who stores, processes or transmits account data, and this course follows version four point zero point one.

10
00:01:00,002 --> 00:01:07,704
Its scope starts with the cardholder data environment, the C D E: every system that stores, processes or transmits that data.

11
00:01:07,754 --> 00:01:13,440
Any system with unrestricted connectivity to those systems belongs to the C D E too.

12
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:21,936
Systems that could impact its security are in scope as well: authentication servers, key stores, logging, and name resolution.

13
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:24,888
Without segmentation, that is the whole network.

14
00:01:24,938 --> 00:01:34,632
Segmentation isolates the C D E behind network security controls, N S Cs, such as firewalls that allow only the flows it needs.

15
00:01:34,682 --> 00:01:39,480
The standard does not require it, but it reduces scope, cost and risk.

16
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:47,232
To be out of scope, a system must be isolated so well that, even if it is compromised, it cannot impact card data.

17
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:56,088
In this system, the C D E is the cards unit: the card frame, the card vault, the BIN module, the adapters, and their infrastructure.

18
00:01:56,138 --> 00:01:57,816
This course calls it the card zone.

19
00:01:57,866 --> 00:02:04,992
The card zone shares nothing: it runs its own Postgres, Vault, Keycloak and observability, separate from ecom's.

20
00:02:05,042 --> 00:02:07,080
Anything it shared would be in scope too.

21
00:02:07,130 --> 00:02:08,952
Only two doors are open.

22
00:02:09,002 --> 00:02:13,536
Cards ingress is the door for payers' browsers, which load the card frame.

23
00:02:13,586 --> 00:02:17,832
Cards partner is the door for callers in other units, such as ecom.

24
00:02:17,882 --> 00:02:24,600
The card database is reachable from nowhere outside the unit, and lesson twelve covers both doors in detail.

25
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:30,912
ecom never stores, processes or transmits a card number, so it is not in the C D E.

26
00:02:30,962 --> 00:02:39,648
It is still in scope, as a connected-to system and a security-impacting one: it calls the card zone, and its payment page embeds the card frame.

27
00:02:39,698 --> 00:02:45,312
The card zone shrinks ecom's scope without removing it, and lesson five draws that line.

28
00:02:45,362 --> 00:02:48,984
The standard groups twelve requirements under six goals.

29
00:02:49,034 --> 00:02:52,272
Every later lesson names the requirements it meets.

30
00:02:52,322 --> 00:02:54,696
Who meets them is split three ways.

31
00:02:54,746 --> 00:03:00,600
The product meets some in code: card numbers are sealed by Vault Transit before they reach Postgres.

32
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:06,744
The bank meets others with its own zone, such as the physical security of the host and its media.

33
00:03:06,794 --> 00:03:14,232
Some are shared: Vault opens with three of five unseal shares, and the bank makes sure no one holds two.

34
00:03:14,282 --> 00:03:23,280
The bank's Qualified Security Assessor, a Q S A, judges all of it inside the bank's assessment, and from here on this course says the assessor.

35
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:29,064
The product claims no certificate of its own, and lesson thirty-eight walks the full matrix.

36
00:03:29,114 --> 00:03:31,560
Now check the boundary on your own laptop.

37
00:03:31,610 --> 00:03:38,448
The implementation guide lists every flow the unit needs, and the unit uses nothing else, in or out.

38
00:03:38,498 --> 00:03:44,352
In: payers' browsers on the public port, callers' hosts on the door port, and the bank's jump host.

39
00:03:44,402 --> 00:03:50,856
Out: the card processing centres, the image registries, the mail relay, and the repository.

40
00:03:50,906 --> 00:03:58,848
Docker Compose publishes exactly those two doors, each on the address the environment names, and stops if the value is missing.

41
00:03:58,898 --> 00:04:05,832
The consoles, Keycloak, Mailpit and Grafana, listen on one two seven, zero, zero, one only.

42
00:04:05,882 --> 00:04:11,136
Postgres publishes no port at all: card data is read through the card vault only.

43
00:04:11,186 --> 00:04:18,048
Behind the payers' door, engine X passes six routes to the card vault and answers four oh four to everything else.

44
00:04:18,098 --> 00:04:26,952
They are the entry page, the frame's script and style sheet, the C S P report, and the card and code steps, and lesson twelve explains each one.

45
00:04:27,002 --> 00:04:31,440
With both units running from lesson one, list what each service publishes.

46
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:40,536
Two doors on the loopback address, eight four four three and nine four four three, the consoles on loopback too, and Postgres with only its container port.

47
00:04:40,586 --> 00:04:44,424
Then ask the payers' door for the frame script, and for anything else.

48
00:04:44,474 --> 00:04:50,640
The frame answers two hundred, and the health endpoint answers four oh four, because the door does not pass it.

49
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:56,496
The installer's check command runs the same two probes, plus every service's health.

50
00:04:56,546 --> 00:05:05,088
Last, the guide's own table sorts every requirement into product, bank and shared, and rows three and nine hold the examples you just heard.

51
00:05:05,138 --> 00:05:11,904
You now have a card zone you have checked yourself: two doors, six routes, and a database nobody outside can reach.

52
00:05:11,954 --> 00:05:15,552
Next: the two units, and the contract between them.
